I'm going to save their habitat.
我要去拯救它们的栖息地。

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:33.982
And it's kind of this philosophical debate of, is it possible the older you get into adulthood?
这其实是一个哲学辩论：当你步入成年后，这真的可能吗？

00:09:33.982 --> 00:09:39.239
And I agree with you completely that your late 20s, you're maybe an adult.
我完全同意你的观点，二十多岁末期，你可能才算是个成年人。

00:09:39.239 --> 00:09:44.212
It depends on the person, but I feel like I became an adult in my 30s.
这因人而异，但我感觉自己是在三十多岁才真正成为成年人的。

00:09:44.212 --> 00:09:47.125
Honestly, isn't it the prefrontal cortex?
老实说，这难道不是前额叶皮层的问题吗？

00:09:47.125 --> 00:09:48.688
It only settles at 25.
它要到25岁才会发育成熟。

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:51.032
You're still something like that.
你还处于那个阶段之类的。

00:09:51.032 --> 00:09:51.532
Yeah.
是啊。

00:09:51.532 --> 00:09:52.032
Yeah.
没错。

00:09:52.032 --> 00:09:53.879
And it's like, here he is.
你看他现在这样。

00:09:53.879 --> 00:10:02.405
Marshall, he's a 27 or something and he's headed into now all his life is going to get expensive all of a sudden, right?
马歇尔，他27岁左右，他现在面临着生活突然变得昂贵起来，对吧？

00:10:02.405 --> 00:10:06.099
They're in debt from the suit and the wedding dress.
他们因为西装和婚纱背负了债务。

00:10:06.099 --> 00:10:30.964
They have a wedding coming up and the future and kids and all of a sudden when faced with all of that, he's, he's, now it's the first time he's really being tested about, do you mean this thing that you said you wanted to do when you were in grade school when you saw this woman gave this very sobering assessment that the gorillas would all be dead.
他们即将举行婚礼，还要考虑未来和孩子，突然面对这一切，这是他第一次真正受到考验：你是否还坚持你在小学时看到那个女人给出令人清醒的评估——大猩猩即将灭绝时，你所说的那种想做的事？

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:31.529
Yeah.
是啊。

00:10:31.529 --> 00:10:41.409
It's not the most inspiring pep talk he got, but it really landed for him and he's such an empath and a sweetheart and a compassionate guy.
那不是他听过最励志的动员讲话，但对他触动很大，他是个非常有同理心、心地善良且富有同情心的人。

00:10:41.409 --> 00:10:42.649
That is his mission.
那是他的使命。

00:10:42.649 --> 00:10:44.169
And now this is the first real test.
而现在这是第一次真正的考验。

00:10:44.169 --> 00:10:49.929
This is the first real we're going to kick the tires, we're going to come at this idea a little bit.
这是第一次我们要去试探，我们要去挑战这个想法。

00:10:49.929 --> 00:10:51.450
The real life is going to come for you.
现实生活要找上门来了。

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.009
Can you hold on to this thing?
你还能坚持住那个理想吗？

00:10:53.009 --> 00:10:55.918
image of who you thought you'd be as a grown-up.
那个你以为自己长大后会成为的样子。

00:10:55.918 --> 00:10:57.735
And I really like that debate.
我真的很喜欢这个辩论。

00:10:57.735 --> 00:11:01.552
I really like that it's an episode that's primarily about that.
我真的很喜欢这一集主要就是围绕这个展开的。

00:11:01.552 --> 00:11:05.429
It's not primarily about dating and love and relationship stuff.
它不是主要讲约会、爱情和人际关系。

00:11:05.429 --> 00:11:06.156
Who are you?
你是谁？

00:11:06.156 --> 00:11:11.124
Can you hold on to that best version of yourself you thought you were going to be?
你还能坚持住你以为自己会成为的那个最好的自己吗？

00:11:11.124 --> 00:11:20.030
It's also, though, it does brush up against dating and relationship and marriage, because he says, well, how am I going to provide for this family?
不过，它确实也触及了约会、关系和婚姻，因为他说，好吧，我该怎么养活这个家庭？

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:29.784
How am I going to, you know, my wife is a kindergarten teacher, like what kind of salaries together, what kind of life will that buy us in New York City no less.
我该怎么……你知道，我妻子是幼儿园老师，我们俩加起来是什么样的薪水，在纽约市这种地方能过什么样的生活。

00:11:29.784 --> 00:11:33.480
So it does brush up against, you know, the economic question.
所以它确实触及了经济问题。

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:36.751
This is actually the other thing I wanted to bring up.
这其实是我想要提出的另一件事。

00:11:36.751 --> 00:11:43.597
I read a really great article in The Atlantic by Sophie Gilbert this week about essentially like economics on TV.
本周我读了索菲·吉尔伯特在《大西洋月刊》上写的一篇很棒的文章，主要讲的是电视里的经济状况。

00:11:43.597 --> 00:11:46.929
And she asked like, when did TV becomes so filthy rich.
她问，电视是从什么时候开始变得如此富得流油的。

00:11:46.929 --> 00:11:54.330
Like when did TV, right now, TV isn't really in a moment where everyone, money's not an issue right now on TV.
电视现在所处的时刻，钱在电视里根本不是个问题。

00:11:54.330 --> 00:12:02.690
Everyone has an enormous amount of money to burn vacations to go to where people get murdered and beach houses.
每个人都有花不完的钱去度假，去那些会发生谋杀案的地方，还有海滨别墅。

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:07.250
And it's just like money is no object on TV right now.
现在的电视里钱根本不是事儿。

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:16.090
And she bemoans this and I think rightly that if you want TV to be a reflect, I mean, it's a tricky thing because you don't want TV to be a downer.
她对此表示遗憾，我认为是对的，如果你想让电视成为一种反映……这很棘手，因为你不想让电视变得太压抑。

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:18.210
TV is still an escape, right?
电视仍然是一种逃避，对吧？

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:28.330
Like you still wanna go somewhere where you can get away from stuff, but you also don't wanna feel like you're in like a gilded palace that you don't relate to.
你还是想去一个能让你远离现实的地方，但你也不想感觉自己身处一个无法产生共鸣的镀金宫殿里。

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:34.970
And I've always admired, something I admired about this show is economics is at play.
我一直很欣赏这部剧的一点是，经济因素在其中发挥了作用。

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:39.409
And Barney is planning these seeds of doubt about like what a man is, right?
巴尼正在播下怀疑的种子，关于男人是什么，对吧？

00:12:39.409 --> 00:12:42.090
It's a man's job to provide everything for the woman.
男人有责任为女人提供一切，对吧？

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:44.409
is very kind of old school, right?
这非常老派，对吧？

00:12:44.409 --> 00:12:54.409
Lily Yesher, Lily's a kindergarten teacher, but deep down she wants the package, according to Barney, which led to some great word play Dick jokes that I really enjoy later.
莉莉是幼儿园老师，但巴尼认为，她内心深处想要那种“全套服务”，这导致了后来一些我很喜欢的关于“丁丁”的俏皮话。

00:12:54.409 --> 00:13:12.169
But there really is legitimate content into what he's saying, which is he's saying, she may say one thing, but deep down, and Barney's playing to that kind of primal sort of caveman, like very deep primordial thing that men feel, I think of, I've got to be this provider.
但他所说的话确实有合理的成分，他在说，她嘴上可能说一套，但内心深处……巴尼是在利用那种原始的、穴居人式的、男人内心深处非常原始的感觉，即“我必须成为供养者”。

00:13:12.169 --> 00:13:23.250
And he really fucks with Marshall's head for lack of a better term and starts, plans a seat of doubt that, yes, Lily will, maybe she's not now, but she will be the woman who will want all of those things.
他确实把马歇尔搞糊涂了，开始播下怀疑的种子，即莉莉虽然现在可能不是这样，但她终究会成为那种想要这一切的女人。

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:32.309
Even as Lily is saying to Marshall, I don't need those things, I want you to be yourself and you're sliding into becoming someone else and I don't like it, but it's so powerful.
尽管莉莉对马歇尔说，我不需要那些东西，我希望你做你自己，你正在变成另一个人，我不喜欢这样，但这太有影响力了。

00:13:32.309 --> 00:13:43.602
I think that pull of what you think you're supposed to be or do as a man, and I think we are really witnessing to get to political sociological at the moment.
我认为那种关于你作为男人应该成为什么样的人或做什么事的拉扯，我们现在确实正在见证，虽然不想在这个时候变得太政治化或社会学化。

00:13:43.602 --> 00:13:52.607
But I think we're really witnessing in our culture right now, 2025, as opposed to 2006, although it's kind of the same debate.
但我认为我们现在在2025年的文化中确实正在见证这一点，而不是2006年，尽管辩论内容差不多。

00:13:52.607 --> 00:13:54.108
What is a man, right?
什么是男人，对吧？

00:13:54.108 --> 00:13:55.608
What is a man's role?
男人的角色是什么？

00:13:55.608 --> 00:13:57.752
How does a man define himself?
男人如何定义自己？

00:13:57.752 --> 00:14:04.113
And I think we were seeing some very wrong answers to that question, if I may be so bold.
如果我可以直言不讳的话，我认为我们正在看到对这个问题的一些非常错误的回答。

00:14:04.113 --> 00:14:12.689
And we see Marshall get seduced buy some of those, maybe the sketchier version of what a man thinks he's supposed to be.
我们看到马歇尔被其中一些观点所诱惑，也许是关于男人“应该”成为什么样的人的更糟糕的版本。

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:18.259
It almost feels like you guys were a little ahead of what's going on now, like Blauman and Billson.
感觉你们好像比现在发生的事情领先了一点，比如布劳曼和比尔森。

00:14:18.259 --> 00:14:19.384
Is that their names?
是叫这个名字吗？

00:14:19.384 --> 00:14:20.509
Blauman and Billson?
布劳曼和比尔森？

00:14:20.509 --> 00:14:21.747
Brian and Taryn, yeah.
布莱恩和塔伦，是的。

00:14:21.747 --> 00:14:24.672
What perfect names, by the way, Blauman and Billson.
顺便说一句，布劳曼和比尔森，多完美的名字。

00:14:24.672 --> 00:14:29.736
Those names came from pretend names we used to make up with Carl McLaren and Jamie Adamek.
这些名字来自我们以前和卡尔·麦克拉伦、杰米·阿达梅克一起编造的假名字。

00:14:29.736 --> 00:14:38.849
We would just make up these middle manager kind of guys and those were like the names of the guys that were like characters we would sometimes just pretend to do.
我们只是编造这些中层管理人员，那些名字就是我们有时假装扮演的角色。

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:39.350
Yeah.
是啊。

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:45.872
And so we stole them as a tribute to those bits to put in these sort of like these striving middle management kind of guys.
所以我们把它们偷过来作为对那些段子的致敬，用在这些努力奋斗的中层管理人员身上。

00:14:45.872 --> 00:14:50.962
And by the way, the first significant other of a him and man cast member has made an appearance.
顺便说一句，剧组人员的第一位重要伴侣登场了。

00:14:50.962 --> 00:14:51.545
Has landed.
已经着陆了。

00:14:51.545 --> 00:14:56.583
Taren Kilum, Coby's friend, then Fiancé and then husband and now father of she'll her children.
塔伦·基勒姆，寇碧的朋友，后来成了未婚夫，然后是丈夫，现在是她孩子们的父亲。

00:14:56.583 --> 00:15:02.309
And there that begins a quick succession in this next batch of episodes of several other significant others.
从那以后，在接下来的几集里，其他几位重要伴侣也接连出现。

00:15:02.309 --> 00:15:03.741
But this was the first one.
但这只是第一个。

00:15:03.741 --> 00:15:06.604
This opened the floodgates and then we got to come on.
这打开了闸门，然后我们……得了吧。

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:12.973
We're making up for me not having been married on the show by having Jordana on every episode of this show.
我们通过让乔丹娜出现在每一集里，来弥补我在剧中没有结婚的遗憾。

00:15:12.973 --> 00:15:15.318
Yes, and we finally got, that's right.
是的，我们终于做到了，没错。

00:15:15.318 --> 00:15:17.724
And now this is your significant other.
现在这是你的重要伴侣。

00:15:17.724 --> 00:15:18.774
This is my, yeah.
这是我的，是的。

00:15:18.774 --> 00:15:21.119
So we've we've we've read it the boat.
所以我们已经……我们已经把船划出去了。

00:15:21.119 --> 00:15:33.769
So, but also, I mean, there's a kind of like the culture in that company among those guys, like not to name any names, but it does feel like we could probably guess what podcast they're listening to today.
不过，在那家公司里，在那些人之间，有一种文化，不点名了，但感觉我们大概能猜到他们今天在听什么播客。

00:15:33.769 --> 00:15:42.889
It feels like they were in the man-a-sphere before there was such a thing, or they were like waiting for their cultural moment to come along.
感觉他们在“大男子主义圈”出现之前就已经身处其中了，或者他们一直在等待属于他们的文化时刻到来。

00:15:42.889 --> 00:15:46.330
Those guys have a podcast at this point.
那些家伙现在都有播客了。

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:47.330
They have a podcast.
他们有播客。

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:51.850
They have a very mid podcast.
他们的播客非常平庸。

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.690
It's the blam and dulcen hour, and it is really fucking crazy.
那是《布劳曼和比尔森时间》，真的太疯狂了。

00:15:55.690 --> 00:16:05.929
But also think of it, just the culture pleasure of Marshall Erickson from pretty liberal Minnesota Wesleyan graduate entering into this thing.
但也要想想，马歇尔·埃里克森从一个相当自由派的明尼苏达卫斯理大学毕业生进入这个环境，这本身就是一种文化冲击。

00:16:05.929 --> 00:16:13.490
He must have felt, I mean, you see, he feels really underwater and ill-equipped, but he also makes this pivot.
他一定感到……你看，他确实感到非常不知所措，装备不足，但他还是做出了转变。

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:25.683
But I just think there's something so refreshing about seeing people think in your light, everyone's economic situation is affecting their choices, it's affecting whether they're going to meet their friend for dinner.
但我只是觉得，看到人们像你一样思考是非常令人耳目一新的，每个人的经济状况都在影响他们的选择，影响他们是否要去见朋友吃晚饭。

00:16:25.683 --> 00:16:29.167
If they have enough money, it's affecting where they can live.
如果他们有足够的钱，这会影响他们能住在哪里。

00:16:29.167 --> 00:16:37.147
It's affecting what cities they're going to, what jobs they're going to take, like economics, especially in America, such a driver of so much.
这会影响他们去哪个城市，从事什么工作，经济，尤其是在美国，是如此巨大的驱动力。

00:16:37.147 --> 00:16:41.811
And I feel like it's a dereliction of duty when TV shirks looking directly at that.
我觉得如果电视回避直接审视这一点，那就是一种失职。

00:16:41.811 --> 00:16:42.597
Yeah, I agree.
是的，我同意。

00:16:42.597 --> 00:16:46.081
I really like seeing them under legitimate financial pressure.
我真的很喜欢看他们承受合法的经济压力。

00:16:46.081 --> 00:17:02.490
And it's kind kind of changing who Marshall is and its decisions he's making because we've all been there in some way, where like, I mean, I remember like looking at life through that lens, like in season one of how I met your mother, like we, you know, we were all on the climb, right, Josh?
这在某种程度上改变了马歇尔的为人以及他所做的决定，因为我们都曾以某种方式经历过这种情况，就像我记得在《老爸老妈浪漫史》第一季中，我们都是在攀登高峰，对吧，乔什？

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:08.450
We were all like, we were ambitious and hungry and we wanted this thing to work and this was our ticket, right?
我们当时都很有野心，渴望成功，我们想要这件事情成功，这就是我们的入场券，对吧？

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:19.210
And we're chasing this thing and you're maybe like, deprioritizing other parts of your life and brain and health and whatever else is going on to like this kind of workaholic focus.
我们在追逐这个目标，你可能正在牺牲生活中其他部分，比如大脑、健康或其他正在发生的事情，转而投入到这种工作狂式的专注中。

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:24.809
You know, I remember thinking, I'm married, I've been married for years, we're probably gonna have a kid soon.
你知道，我记得当时想，我已经结婚了，结婚好几年了，我们可能很快就会有孩子。

00:17:24.809 --> 00:17:28.009
And like all of these issues were in my brain too.
所有这些问题也都在我的脑海里。

00:17:28.009 --> 00:17:38.130
And you know, and it's a very powerful thing to, I think for men, I mean, I don't wanna, I'm a man, so I'll only talk about it from that perspective.
你知道，我认为这对男人来说是一件非常有影响力的事情，我是说，我不想……我是一个男人，所以我只会从那个角度来谈论它。

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:42.130
But what Marshall gets seduced by there is very real.
但马歇尔在那里被诱惑的东西是非常真实的。

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:49.890
And you're coming up on 30, you're in your late 20s and your 30s, all of a sudden you're looking ahead and you're thinking about children in the future.
你快30岁了，处于20多岁末期和30岁出头，突然间你展望未来，开始考虑未来的孩子。

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:53.769
And I really, I bought Marshall's kind of slide into the seduction.
我真的很相信马歇尔那种陷入诱惑的过程。

00:17:53.769 --> 00:17:59.089
Obviously it's funny and it's silly and me maybe tried to make it amusing, but I think it's pretty real, right?
显然这很有趣，也很傻，我可能试图让它变得好玩，但我认为这是相当真实的，对吧？

00:17:59.089 --> 00:18:13.435
That even a guy with all of those kind of values who is a cuddly, sweet, empathetic bear of a guy, there is something seductive when someone whispers in your ear and goes like deep down your wife wants you to be rich.
即使是一个拥有所有这些价值观、像泰迪熊一样可爱、贴心、善解人意的家伙，当有人在你耳边低语，说“其实你老婆内心深处希望你变得富有”时，还是会有某种诱惑力。

00:18:13.435 --> 00:18:19.319
Your wife wants everything, but not only that, there's a gravitational pull to a culture.
你老婆想要一切，不仅如此，对一种文化还有一种引力。

00:18:19.319 --> 00:18:28.509
I know a woman who moved down to Florida kind of mid-pandemic with her husband and her kids, and she was very kind of New York progressive.
我认识一个女人，她在疫情中期和丈夫孩子搬到了佛罗里达，她之前是非常典型的纽约进步派。

00:18:28.509 --> 00:18:33.136
Her politics were just like line drive kind of New York City politics.
她的政治立场就像纽约市的政治路线一样直接。

00:18:33.136 --> 00:18:48.937
And she moved down to Florida, and she was surrounded by different political beliefs and she said like I still consider myself on the left end of the spectrum but it changed a lot of my thinking just being saturated in a different culture.
她搬到佛罗里达后，被不同的政治信仰所包围，她说，虽然我仍然认为自己处于左翼光谱，但沉浸在不同的文化中确实改变了我很多想法。

00:18:48.937 --> 00:18:56.672
So Marshall like steps into this culture and it rattles him because he also doesn't want to get made fun of at lunch.
所以马歇尔踏入这种文化，这让他感到不安，因为他也不想在午餐时被嘲笑。

00:18:56.672 --> 00:18:58.589
He doesn't want to eat alone.
他不想一个人吃饭。

00:18:58.589 --> 00:19:02.490
He doesn't want to feel like like he's the lame kid, right?
他不想觉得自己是个没用的家伙，对吧？

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:08.849
And it's funny how at a lesson, fear of being left out never quite leaves us, right?
有趣的是，恐惧被排斥的感觉从未真正离开过我们，对吧？

00:19:08.849 --> 00:19:11.170
