00:36:14.339 --> 00:36:14.839
Oh?
哦？

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:15.989
Ted's?
Ted的？

00:36:15.989 --> 00:36:16.989
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:36:16.989 --> 00:36:19.789
Were you literally falling asleep on the couch next to Jordana?
你当时是在沙发上靠着Jordana睡着了吗？

00:36:19.789 --> 00:36:23.789
You were doing the Ted's band, and I'm just writing nonsense down.
你在做Ted的乐队，而我只是在写些胡言乱语。

00:36:23.789 --> 00:36:24.789
Yeah.
是的。

00:36:24.789 --> 00:36:25.789
Ted's sure.
Ted的衬衫。

00:36:25.789 --> 00:36:26.789
I'm fine with Robin.
我和Robin在一起很好。

00:36:26.789 --> 00:36:27.789
Oh, I like some of these notes.
哦，我喜欢其中一些笔记。

00:36:27.789 --> 00:36:29.789
Like every third one, you're like, what in God's name?
每隔三条，你就会写“天哪，这是什么？”

00:36:29.789 --> 00:36:30.789
What is that?
那是什么？

00:36:30.789 --> 00:36:31.789
12 hours ago.
12小时前。

00:36:31.789 --> 00:36:32.789
When I watched this.
当我看这一集的时候。

00:36:32.789 --> 00:36:33.789
I know.
我知道。

00:36:33.789 --> 00:36:38.710
I did think dirty deeds done dirt cheap was the perfect song for Barney to sing.
我确实认为《Dirty Deeds Done Dirt Cheap》是Barney演唱的完美歌曲。

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:40.190
That's like his go to perfect.
那是他的招牌，完美。

00:36:40.190 --> 00:36:41.590
So I forgot.
所以我忘了。

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:42.590
I had forgot.
我忘了。

00:36:42.590 --> 00:36:52.867
forgot that was Barney's karaoke song until I was scouring new songs we should play for our How I Met Your Mother fundraiser concert that we do at Valerie Ballroom that will be another one in early 2026.
忘了那是Barney的卡拉OK歌曲，直到我在搜寻我们应该为2026年初在Valerie Ballroom举办的《老爸老妈浪漫史》筹款音乐会演奏的新歌时。

00:36:52.867 --> 00:36:55.348
And I was like, what song should Will Forte sing?
当时我在想，Will Forte应该唱什么歌？

00:36:55.348 --> 00:36:56.715
Neil couldn't come to that.
Neil没法参加那次活动。

00:36:56.715 --> 00:36:59.399
It couldn't perform with us if that he was traveling.
如果他在旅行，就没法和我们一起表演。

00:36:59.399 --> 00:37:05.424
And we knew we had Will Forte coming in and we're like, what song can Will Forte just scream the living bejesus out of?
我们知道Will Forte要来，我们在想，Will Forte能用什么歌声嘶力竭地唱出来？

00:37:05.424 --> 00:37:08.765
And I was like, we're looking around and it's like, it's that one.
然后我们环顾四周，就是那首。

00:37:08.765 --> 00:37:11.195
Because we only play at that fundraiser concert.
因为我们只在那场筹款音乐会上演奏。

00:37:11.195 --> 00:37:16.562
We only play songs that are either like written for how much mother or were on the soundtrack or featured.
我们只演奏那些为《老爸老妈浪漫史》创作的，或者在原声带里，或者在剧中出现过的歌曲。

00:37:16.562 --> 00:37:18.739
And so that was thrilling to discover that.
所以发现这一点很令人兴奋。

00:37:18.739 --> 00:37:22.789
And Will Forte came out, sang it with Carter, and killed, killed with that song.
Will Forte上台，和Carter一起唱了这首歌，效果炸裂，那首歌太棒了。

00:37:22.789 --> 00:37:24.989
And Neil killed it too.
Neil也唱得很棒。

00:37:24.989 --> 00:37:27.483
So let's just talk about the ending.
所以，让我们谈谈结局。

00:37:27.483 --> 00:37:33.369
I just thought it was so brilliant, I'd forgotten one that, you know, what do you do?
我觉得太精彩了，我忘了……你知道，该怎么办？

00:37:33.369 --> 00:37:34.338
Go comes back.
Go回来了。

00:37:34.338 --> 00:37:38.355
But I was really struck by how Ted is clearly the B story.
但我真的很震惊Ted显然是B线故事。

00:37:38.355 --> 00:37:42.787
Ted and Robin and this Victoria thing is so clearly the B story.
Ted和Robin以及Victoria的这段戏显然是B线故事。

00:37:42.787 --> 00:37:49.227
But at the very last moment, it just like tees it up as like, now we're going to follow this.
但在最后一刻，它就像是把它架起来，说“现在我们要关注这个了”。

00:37:49.227 --> 00:37:54.213
Like this is going to, this is now the eight story for the next episode.
这成了下一集的A线故事。

00:37:54.213 --> 00:37:56.498
And it's such a good cliffhanger.
这是一个非常好的悬念。

00:37:56.498 --> 00:38:02.870
I think by episode 17 into 18, you've got some real emotional investment among the audience.
我认为从第17集到第18集，观众已经有了真正的感情投入。

00:38:03.989 --> 00:38:07.429
I thought it was one of the better cliffhangers of episode of season one.
我觉得这是第一季中最好的悬念之一。

00:38:07.429 --> 00:38:09.389
Thanks, I love that moment.
谢谢，我很喜欢那一刻。

00:38:09.389 --> 00:38:11.150
It gives me a chill every time that ending.
每次看到那个结局，我都会感到一阵寒意。

00:38:11.150 --> 00:38:19.150
It just gives, there's just when it cuts to black there and there's that little beat even before the credits come in, which we kept there to sort of let it land.
那种感觉就是，当画面切成黑色，在片尾字幕出现前有那么一小段停顿，我们保留了它，是为了让那种情绪沉淀下来。

00:38:20.309 --> 00:38:21.150
I love that.
我喜欢那样。

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:25.829
I love that it sort of sneaks up on you as this dramatic ending and it really spins you into the dilemma of the next one.
我喜欢它作为戏剧性结局悄然降临的方式，它真的把你带入了下一个故事的困境中。

00:38:25.829 --> 00:38:26.869
This is what we love doing.
这就是我们热爱做的事情。

00:38:26.869 --> 00:38:29.190
We loved writing it as one big continuous thing.
我们喜欢把它写成一个完整连贯的故事。

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:30.869
We didn't want it to be a box of chocolates.
我们不想让它像一盒巧克力那样（零散）。

00:38:30.869 --> 00:38:34.909
We didn't want it to be standalone episode, standalone episode, you can watch anyone in any order.
我们不想让它成为那种可以随意观看、顺序不限的独立剧集。

00:38:34.909 --> 00:38:38.510
And, you know, the network was nervous about that.
而且，你知道，电视网对此感到紧张。

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:42.590
We got kind of a talking to you at the end of season one that we're doing too much of that kind of thing.
第一季结束时，我们被“谈话”过，说我们做得太过火了。

00:38:42.590 --> 00:38:43.869
This was before streaming.
那是在流媒体出现之前。

00:38:43.869 --> 00:38:44.829
This was before Netflix.
那是在Netflix崛起之前。

00:38:44.829 --> 00:38:53.949
Netflix was a place that sent you DVDs and red pouches at this point, 2005, six, and people didn't consume the show this way.
那时候的Netflix还是个给你寄DVD和红色封套的地方，那是2005、2006年，人们不是这样看剧的。

00:38:53.949 --> 00:38:56.030
And we were like, but they do.
而我们当时的想法是：但他们确实会这样看。

00:38:56.030 --> 00:39:07.309
They'll get it on the DVD and they'll watch all of it in a row or like, or by the way, why not wait, and now you have to wait a week to see what Ted's gonna do with that amazing dilemma that's just been presented to him.
他们会拿到DVD，然后一口气看完，或者，顺便说一句，为什么不等等呢？现在你必须等上一周，才能看到泰德将如何处理那个刚刚摆在他面前的惊人困境。

00:39:07.309 --> 00:39:14.510
So let people sweat it out for a week and come back as opposed to like, I forget what happened last week on that How Much Mother show.
所以让观众为之揪心一周再回来，而不是像那种“我忘了上周那部《老爸老妈浪漫史》演了什么”的状态。

00:39:14.510 --> 00:39:15.670
I don't see the downside.
我不觉得这有什么坏处。

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:20.190
I guess it excludes people jumping in the middle, but does it really, I would argue it doesn't.
我想这可能会把中途加入的观众拒之门外，但真的会吗？我认为不会。

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:24.110
I mean, it's always essentially an argument about how much do you trust your audience?
我的意思是，这本质上始终是一个关于你有多信任观众的问题。

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:26.150
Yes, and we trusted our audience.
是的，我们信任我们的观众。

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:29.349
And God damn it, it paid off.
该死，这真的得到了回报。

00:39:29.349 --> 00:39:34.253
We're proven right by the smartest and best fans any TV show in history?
我们被电视史上最聪明、最棒的粉丝证明是正确的，不是吗？

00:39:34.253 --> 00:39:47.398
Well, we are at a segment of the show that we like to call questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met your mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
好了，现在到了我们节目中一个喜欢的环节，我们称之为“来自一位从未看过《老爸老妈浪漫史》且恰好是乔什妻子的临床心理学家的提问与观察”。

00:39:47.398 --> 00:39:56.320
I love in this episode how Lily is so encouraging of Marshall following his dreams of not selling out, of not just being practical.
我很喜欢这一集中莉莉对马歇尔的鼓励，支持他追随梦想，而不是为了现实而妥协。

00:39:56.320 --> 00:40:13.006
And it reminded me of a story about John Gottman, who's a well-known couple's therapist and researcher, who apparently when he was trying to publish his first book was rejected multiple times until one guy said, you want me to publish your book?
这让我想起了一个关于约翰·戈特曼的故事，他是一位著名的夫妻治疗师和研究员，据说他在试图出版第一本书时多次被拒，直到有个人说：“你想让我出版你的书？现在告诉我，我该如何修复我和妻子的婚姻？”

00:40:13.006 --> 00:40:13.823
Tell me now.
告诉我。

00:40:13.823 --> 00:40:16.411
How do I fix my marriage with my wife?
我该如何修复我和妻子的婚姻？

00:40:16.411 --> 00:40:20.634
And John Gottman apparently said, ask her what her dreams are.
约翰·戈特曼当时回答说：“问问她的梦想是什么。”

00:40:20.634 --> 00:40:21.587
And it worked.
结果奏效了。

00:40:21.587 --> 00:40:22.745
He published him.
他出版了那本书。

00:40:22.745 --> 00:40:33.165
And the story always stuck with me because I think it highlights how fundamental it is to find a partner who really encourages you to follow your dreams.
这个故事一直印在我的脑海里，因为它强调了找到一个真正鼓励你追随梦想的伴侣是多么重要。

00:40:33.165 --> 00:40:40.112
And I just love how the solid hymium couple here is the one that is supportive of each other's dreams.
我真的很喜欢剧中这对稳固的夫妻，他们是彼此梦想的支持者。

00:40:40.112 --> 00:40:46.242
So my question to you guys is who was supportive of your dreams in childhood and also now.
所以我想问你们的问题是，在童年时期以及现在，是谁在支持你们的梦想？

00:40:46.242 --> 00:40:49.920
What a good that's a great story and a great question.
这真是一个好故事，也是一个好问题。

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:54.159
What do you do you go, Josh, your first one?
你要先来吗，乔什？

00:40:54.159 --> 00:41:07.280
Well, I feel like as an actor and as a person who makes things and tells stories like, there were some formative people in my life along the way.
嗯，我觉得作为一个演员，作为一个创造事物和讲述故事的人，我生命中确实有一些关键人物。

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:19.320
I had and continue to have a friend named David Brown who choreographed the first musical I was in and I think he might have been the first person who told me I could do this professionally.
我有一位名叫大卫·布朗的朋友，他为我参演的第一部音乐剧编舞，我想他可能是第一个告诉我我可以以此为职业的人。

00:41:19.320 --> 00:41:28.559
And then I was at Vassar at a training program one summer, New York stage and film, and two great actors, Jane Kasmerich.
后来我在瓦萨学院参加了一个暑期培训项目，在纽约舞台与电影学院，遇到了两位伟大的演员，简·卡兹马雷克。

00:41:28.559 --> 00:41:32.360
She played the mom on Malcolm in the Middle and a bunch of other stuff, such a good actor.
她在《马尔柯姆的一家》里演妈妈，还演过很多其他作品，是非常优秀的演员。

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:36.760
And this other just incredible actor, Peter Fershette, I was friends with both of them.
还有另一位不可思议的演员彼得·弗谢特，我和他们俩都是朋友。

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:39.599
And they both pulled me aside and said, you can do this.
他们都把我拉到一边说：“你可以做到。”

00:41:39.599 --> 00:41:41.840
You're going to be one of the people here who can do this.
“你会成为这里能做到这一点的人之一。”

00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:51.280
And, you know, there was a mentor I had in school, Ken Washington, who died a couple years ago and at his memorial, I spoke.
而且，你知道，我在学校里有一位导师肯·华盛顿，他几年前去世了，我在他的追悼会上发了言。

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:54.920
It was a bunch of Juilliard and NYU people who were really affected by him.
当时有很多朱莉亚学院和纽约大学的人都深受他的影响。

00:41:54.920 --> 00:41:57.760
We had a kind of makeshift memorial at NYU.
我们在纽约大学举行了一个临时的追悼会。

00:41:57.760 --> 00:42:07.440
And I got up and spoke and I said, there were times when I didn't believe that I could be an actor, but I believed that Ken believed I could be an actor.
我站起来发言说，曾经有那么一段时间，我不相信自己能成为一名演员，但我相信肯相信我能成为一名演员。

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:19.400
And I think sometimes when you don't believe in yourself, there's like, you almost have to have like an external hard drive in the form of a person who holds the faith, like is the faith keeper for you until you can hold it yourself.
我认为有时候当你对自己失去信心时，你几乎需要一个“外接硬盘”，也就是某个人，他为你保留着这份信念，直到你自己能够承担为止。

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:30.519
And so I had an enormous amount of those people over the years that even now, sometimes when I'm feeling down about this or that, there's people that I call because they're so gifted at pep talks.
所以多年来我拥有很多这样的人，即使现在，有时当我因为某事感到沮丧时，我还会给这些人打电话，因为他们太擅长给人打气了。

00:42:30.519 --> 00:42:35.139
My friend, John Morrow is like, just so he's just ready to give me the best pep talk.
我的朋友约翰·莫罗就是这样，他总是准备好给我最好的鼓励。

00:42:35.139 --> 00:42:37.039
And I always feel altered after talking with him.
每次和他交谈后，我总感觉自己有所改变。

00:42:37.039 --> 00:42:43.119
And you know, my wife, who also happens to be a clinical psychologist who's never seen Helen met your mother.
你知道，我妻子也是一位临床心理学家，她从未见过海伦，也没见过你母亲。

00:42:43.119 --> 00:42:45.280
I've heard of her.
我听说过她。

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:46.280
Yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊。

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:49.800
I think we're in her segment right now, I think we're literally in her thing.
我觉得我们现在就在她的领域里，真的，我们就在她的地盘上。

00:42:49.800 --> 00:43:16.930
I mean, in some ways like we weren't together since drama school, like we got together after I was firmly established as doing what I do, but there was no question like she understood in some sort of cellular way, what I do and why I need to do it and her ability to kind of like see what I need to do and what I need to do daily creatively, like there's never been a negotiation around that.
我的意思是，在某种程度上，我们从戏剧学校毕业后并没有在一起，我们是在我确立了职业方向后才走到一起的。但毫无疑问，她从骨子里理解我在做什么，以及我为什么需要这样做。她能够看出我需要做什么，以及我每天在创作上需要完成什么，关于这一点，我们从来不需要协商。

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:21.360
The only negotiation would be, I think you need to do this more.
唯一的协商可能就是，我觉得你应该多做一点。

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:24.250
I think you need to lean into this more.
我觉得你应该在这方面多投入一些。

00:43:24.250 --> 00:43:28.079
I feel really supported in that primary relationship.
在这一核心关系中，我感到非常受支持。

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:29.679
What about you, Craig?
你呢，克雷格？

00:43:29.679 --> 00:43:30.179
Yeah.
是的。

00:43:30.179 --> 00:43:32.408
I like how you just lead out a timeline.
我喜欢你刚才梳理时间线的方式。

00:43:32.408 --> 00:43:34.136
I think that's the truth of it.
我想这就是事实。

00:43:34.136 --> 00:43:38.816
It's like different people at different times, but the earliest ones matter so much.
就像不同时期有不同的人，但最早期的那些人意义重大。

00:43:38.816 --> 00:43:44.221
I would say my My mother and father, my father, who is a writer, creative doctor and advertising.
我会说是我的父母，我父亲，他是一位作家，也是一位从事广告业的创意医生。

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:45.340
And smoker.
还是个烟民。

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:51.340
And big smoker, very mad man era, like an advertising smoker guy.
是个大烟民，非常有《广告狂人》那个时代的风格，就是那种广告界的烟民形象。

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:56.019
And around here and now, he's alive and probably smoking.
现在他还活着，可能还在抽烟。

00:43:56.019 --> 00:43:59.699
If he's listening to this, which I'm not sure he knows, I listen to a podcast.
如果他在听这个节目的话，我不确定他知不知道我听播客。

00:43:59.699 --> 00:44:04.420
But he would just give me cool writers to read.
但他总是会给我推荐很酷的作家让我读。

00:44:04.420 --> 00:44:05.900
That was his way of showing his belief in me.
那是他表达对我有信心的方式。

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:07.980
Like, read this, read this, read this.
比如，读这个，读这个，读这个。

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:08.860
You should read it.
你应该读读看。

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:11.383
here's John Shiver, here's Raymond Carver, here's J.D.
这是约翰·奇弗，这是雷蒙德·卡佛，这是J.D.

00:44:11.383 --> 00:44:18.019
Soundger, like, and just like, he didn't give me pep talk after pep talking, but he, I knew he believed in me because he told me what to read.
塞林格，诸如此类。他不会给我灌输什么励志鸡汤，但我知道他相信我，因为他会告诉我该读什么。

00:44:18.019 --> 00:44:22.179
And my mother more explicitly would say, you know, saw that I was a good writer.
我母亲更明确地说过，你知道，她看出我是一个优秀的作家。

00:44:22.179 --> 00:44:26.340
She saw my schoolwork, she saw that I could write, and knew that I was thinking about doing it.
她看过我的学校作业，看出我能写作，也知道我在考虑从事这一行。

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:40.329
And when at a very young age, when I would have a hard day and I'd come and I'd talk about something that was terrible at school or something that was bothering me, or, you know, my parents divorcing early in high school, she would say, well, it's all grist for your mill.
在我很小的时候，每当我度过了艰难的一天，回到家谈起学校里发生的糟糕事，或者什么让我烦恼的事，又或者是我父母在高中早期离婚的事，她总会说，这些都是你创作的素材。

00:44:40.329 --> 00:44:48.550
